/-!
# The parity test for Decisional Diffie–Hellman in groups of even order

In a group of even order `q` the parity of a discrete logarithm is visible: raising an element to
the power `q / 2` gives the identity exactly when its discrete logarithm is even. A Diffie–Hellman
triple `(g^α, g^β, g^(αβ))` therefore satisfies a parity relation that a random triple satisfies
only half of the time. This module proves Lemmas 16.1 and 16.2 of [BonehShoup2023], Section
16.1.3, and the section's sentence that the resulting three-step parity test has advantage `1 / 2`
in breaking DDH, on the fixed-group experiments of
`CslibFrontier/Crypto/Computational/DiffieHellman/Finite.lean`.

The fixed-group advantage `ddhAdvantage` places no bound on the distinguisher: in this model a
brute-force discrete-logarithm distinguisher already has advantage at least `1 - 1 / q`. What
makes the book's attack meaningful is that the test is efficient. That is stated over cyclic group
families in `CslibFrontier/Crypto/Computational/DiffieHellman/CyclicGroupFamily.lean`: the
certificate `CyclicGroupFamily.isPPT_ddhParityDistinguisher` gives one uniform strict PPT machine
that computes the test on encoded inputs, and `CyclicGroupFamily.not_ddh_of_even_order` concludes
that DDH does not hold in a family of even order, which is Exercise 10.24 (p. 431). The book's
conclusion that DDH does not hold in `Z_p^*` (p. 687, and the remark after Exercise 10.24) needs a
family of the groups `(ZMod p)ˣ`, which is not constructed; here it is proved in the fixed-group
form `ddhAdvantage_units_ddhParityDistinguisher`.

The setting is that of `Finite.lean`: an additive commutative group `G` that is a `ZMod q`-module
and an element `g : G`, with the textbook `g^α` written `α • g`. The book's cyclic group of order
`n` generated by `g` enters as injectivity of `c ↦ c • g` on `ZMod q`, that is, `g` has order `q`;
`G` itself need not be generated by `g`, and the random world samples `w` uniformly from the
multiples of `g` (the book's "`w` uniform in `G`" when `g` generates `G`). The results also assume
`Even q`. The book's `u^(n/2) = 1` is `(q / 2) • u = 0`, and its parity of `α ∈ Z_n` is the parity
of the natural representative `α.val`.

## Main definitions

* `Cslib.Crypto.ddhParityDistinguisher`: the distinguisher of Section 16.1.3, which accepts
  `(u, v, w)` when `w` has an even discrete logarithm exactly when `u` or `v` has one.
* `Cslib.Crypto.zmodUnitsModule`: the multiplicative group `(ZMod p)ˣ`, written additively, as a
  `ZMod (p - 1)`-module. It is a definition, not a global instance.

## Main results

* `Cslib.Crypto.div_two_nsmul_smul_eq_zero_iff`: Lemma 16.1, `(q / 2) • (a • g) = 0` exactly when
  `a.val` is even.
* `Cslib.Crypto.even_val_mul_iff`: Lemma 16.2, `(a * b).val` is even exactly when
  `(q / 2) • (a • g) = 0` or `(q / 2) • (b • g) = 0`.
* `Cslib.Crypto.probability_ddhRealExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher`,
  `Cslib.Crypto.probability_ddhRandomExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher`: the distinguisher accepts
  with probability `1` in the real world and `1 / 2` in the random world. The exact output laws
  are `eval_ddhRealExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher` and
  `eval_ddhRandomExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher`.
* `Cslib.Crypto.ddhAdvantage_ddhParityDistinguisher`: the test has fixed-group DDH advantage
  exactly `1 / 2`.
* `Cslib.Crypto.injective_smul_ofMul_units`: a generator of `(ZMod p)ˣ` has order `p - 1`.
* `Cslib.Crypto.ddhAdvantage_units_ddhParityDistinguisher`: in `(ZMod p)ˣ`, `p` an odd prime, with
  any generator, the test has fixed-group DDH advantage exactly `1 / 2`.

The efficiency certificate and Exercise 10.24 are in
`CslibFrontier/Crypto/Computational/DiffieHellman/CyclicGroupFamily.lean`. Not proved: the
asymptotic statement that DDH does not hold in `Z_p^*`, which needs a family of the groups
`(ZMod p)ˣ`, and Exercise 16.2, the generalization to a small prime factor of the order.

## References

* [D. Boneh, V. Shoup, *A Graduate Course in Applied Cryptography*][BonehShoup2023], Section
  16.1.3, Lemmas 16.1 and 16.2 and the advantage of the parity test (p. 687).
-/

public section

namespace Cslib.Crypto

open Probability
open scoped NNReal

/-! ### Parity of discrete logarithms -/

section Parity

variable {G : Type} [AddCommGroup G] {q : ℕ} [NeZero q] [Module (ZMod q) G] {g : G}

/-- A nonzero even natural number is at least `2`. -/
private theorem two_le_of_even (hq : Even q) : 2 ≤ q :=
  Nat.le_of_dvd (Nat.pos_of_ne_zero (NeZero.ne q)) (even_iff_two_dvd.mp hq)

/-- For even `q`, `(q / 2) * a` vanishes in `ZMod q` exactly when `a.val` is even. -/
private theorem natCast_div_two_mul_eq_zero_iff (hq : Even q) (a : ZMod q) :
    ((q / 2 : ℕ) : ZMod q) * a = 0 ↔ Even a.val := by
  have hpos : 0 < q / 2 := Nat.div_pos (two_le_of_even hq) two_pos
  have hcast : ((q / 2 : ℕ) : ZMod q) * a = ((q / 2 * a.val : ℕ) : ZMod q) := by
    rw [Nat.cast_mul, ZMod.natCast_zmod_val]
  rw [hcast, ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff, even_iff_two_dvd]
  calc q ∣ q / 2 * a.val ↔ q / 2 * 2 ∣ q / 2 * a.val := by
        rw [Nat.div_two_mul_two_of_even hq]
    _ ↔ 2 ∣ a.val := Nat.mul_dvd_mul_iff_left hpos

/-- **Lemma 16.1** ([BonehShoup2023], Section 16.1.3, p. 687): for `g` of even order `q`, the
element `a • g` (the textbook `u = g^α`) satisfies `(q / 2) • (a • g) = 0` (the textbook
`u^(n/2) = 1`) exactly when `a.val` is even. The book's cyclic group of order `n` generated by `g`
is here injectivity of `c ↦ c • g` on `ZMod q`; `G` need not be generated by `g`. -/
theorem div_two_nsmul_smul_eq_zero_iff (hq : Even q)
    (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g) (a : ZMod q) :
    (q / 2) • (a • g) = 0 ↔ Even a.val := by
  rw [← Nat.cast_smul_eq_nsmul (ZMod q), smul_smul, hg.eq_iff' (zero_smul (ZMod q) g)]
  exact natCast_div_two_mul_eq_zero_iff hq a

/-- **Lemma 16.2** ([BonehShoup2023], Section 16.1.3, p. 687): for `g` of even order `q`, the
exponent `a * b` of the Diffie–Hellman element is even exactly when `(q / 2) • (a • g) = 0` or
`(q / 2) • (b • g) = 0`. So the parity of the Diffie–Hellman exponent is computable from `a • g`
and `b • g`. -/
theorem even_val_mul_iff (hq : Even q) (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g)
    (a b : ZMod q) :
    Even (a * b).val ↔ (q / 2) • (a • g) = 0 ∨ (q / 2) • (b • g) = 0 := by
  rw [div_two_nsmul_smul_eq_zero_iff hq hg, div_two_nsmul_smul_eq_zero_iff hq hg, ZMod.val_mul,
    Even.mod_even_iff hq, Nat.even_mul]

/-- For even `q`, adding one to an element of `ZMod q` flips the parity of its value. -/
private theorem even_val_add_one_iff (hq : Even q) (c : ZMod q) :
    Even (c + 1).val ↔ ¬Even c.val := by
  rw [ZMod.val_add, ZMod.val_one_eq_one_mod, Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (two_le_of_even hq),
    Even.mod_even_iff hq, Nat.even_add_one]

end Parity

/-! ### The distinguisher -/

/-- The distinguisher of [BonehShoup2023], Section 16.1.3 (p. 687), for a group of even order
`q`: on `(u, v, w)` it computes the parity of the discrete logarithm of `w` by Lemma 16.1 and that
of the Diffie–Hellman exponent by Lemma 16.2, and accepts when they agree, that is, when
`(q / 2) • w = 0` exactly when `(q / 2) • u = 0` or `(q / 2) • v = 0`. It is deterministic. -/
@[expose] def ddhParityDistinguisher {G : Type} [AddMonoid G] [DecidableEq G] (q : ℕ)
    (u v w : G) : ProbComp Bool :=
  pure (decide ((q / 2) • w = 0 ↔ (q / 2) • u = 0 ∨ (q / 2) • v = 0))

/-- A Boolean statistic of a uniform sample is a fair coin when some bijection of the sample space
negates it. -/
private theorem uniformOfFintype_bind_pure_eq_of_flip {α : Type} [Fintype α] [Nonempty α]
    (e : α ≃ α) {E : α → Bool} (hE : ∀ c, E (e c) = !E c) :
    ((PMF.uniformOfFintype α).bind fun c => PMF.pure (E c)) = PMF.uniformOfFintype Bool := by
  have hflip : ((PMF.uniformOfFintype α).bind fun c => PMF.pure (E c)) true =
      ((PMF.uniformOfFintype α).bind fun c => PMF.pure (E c)) false := by
    conv_lhs => rw [← PMF.uniformOfFintype_bind_equiv e]
    simp only [PMF.bind_apply, hE]
    refine tsum_congr fun c => ?_
    cases E c <;> simp [PMF.pure_apply]
  have hsum := PMF.apply_true_add_apply_false
    ((PMF.uniformOfFintype α).bind fun c => PMF.pure (E c))
  rw [← hflip, ← two_mul, mul_comm] at hsum
  have htrue := ENNReal.eq_inv_of_mul_eq_one_left hsum
  ext b
  cases b
  · rw [← hflip, htrue, PMF.uniformOfFintype_apply, Fintype.card_bool, Nat.cast_ofNat]
  · rw [htrue, PMF.uniformOfFintype_apply, Fintype.card_bool, Nat.cast_ofNat]

section Experiments

variable {G : Type} [AddCommGroup G] [DecidableEq G] (q : ℕ) [NeZero q] [Module (ZMod q) G]
  (g : G)

/-- For even `q`, comparing the parity of a uniform `c : ZMod q` with a fixed proposition is a
fair coin: `c ↦ c + 1` flips the parity. -/
private theorem uniformOfFintype_bind_parity (hq : Even q) (P : Prop) [Decidable P] :
    ((PMF.uniformOfFintype (ZMod q)).bind fun c => PMF.pure (decide (Even c.val ↔ P))) =
      PMF.uniformOfFintype Bool :=
  uniformOfFintype_bind_pure_eq_of_flip (Equiv.addRight 1) fun c => by
    simp only [Equiv.coe_addRight, even_val_add_one_iff hq]
    by_cases hc : Even c.val <;> by_cases hP : P <;> simp [hc, hP]

/-- In the real DDH world, `ddhParityDistinguisher` always accepts: by Lemmas 16.1 and 16.2,
both parities it compares are the parity of `a * b`. -/
theorem eval_ddhRealExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher (hq : Even q)
    (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g) :
    ProbComp.eval (ddhRealExperiment q g (ddhParityDistinguisher q)) = PMF.pure true := by
  have h (a b : ZMod q) :
      decide ((q / 2) • ((a * b) • g) = 0 ↔ (q / 2) • (a • g) = 0 ∨ (q / 2) • (b • g) = 0) =
        true :=
    decide_eq_true ((div_two_nsmul_smul_eq_zero_iff hq hg (a * b)).trans
      (even_val_mul_iff hq hg a b))
  simp only [eval_ddhRealExperiment, ddhParityDistinguisher, ProbComp.eval_pure, h,
    PMF.bind_const]

/-- In the random DDH world, `ddhParityDistinguisher` outputs a fair coin: for every `a` and `b`,
the parity of the independent uniform `c` agrees with that of `a * b` with probability `1 / 2`. -/
theorem eval_ddhRandomExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher (hq : Even q)
    (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g) :
    ProbComp.eval (ddhRandomExperiment q g (ddhParityDistinguisher q)) =
      PMF.uniformOfFintype Bool := by
  simp only [eval_ddhRandomExperiment, ddhParityDistinguisher, ProbComp.eval_pure,
    div_two_nsmul_smul_eq_zero_iff hq hg, uniformOfFintype_bind_parity q hq, PMF.bind_const]

/-- The distinguisher of Section 16.1.3 accepts every real DDH triple: its acceptance
probability in `ddhRealExperiment` is `1`. -/
theorem probability_ddhRealExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher (hq : Even q)
    (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g) :
    Pr[ddhRealExperiment q g (ddhParityDistinguisher q)] = 1 := by
  rw [eval_ddhRealExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher q g hq hg, PMF.pure_apply_self,
    ENNReal.toReal_one]

/-- The distinguisher of Section 16.1.3 accepts a random triple with probability `1 / 2`. -/
theorem probability_ddhRandomExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher (hq : Even q)
    (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g) :
    Pr[ddhRandomExperiment q g (ddhParityDistinguisher q)] = 1 / 2 := by
  rw [eval_ddhRandomExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher q g hq hg, PMF.uniformOfFintype_apply,
    Fintype.card_bool, Nat.cast_ofNat, ENNReal.toReal_inv, ENNReal.toReal_ofNat, one_div]

/-- **The parity test has advantage `1 / 2`** ([BonehShoup2023], Section 16.1.3, p. 687): when
`q` is even and `g` has order `q`, the three-step parity test of Section 16.1.3 has fixed-group
DDH advantage exactly `1 / 2`, the book's "this algorithm has advantage 1/2 in breaking DDH in the
group G". The fixed-group advantage does not bound the distinguisher's running time. Exercise
10.24 (p. 431), which asks for an efficient adversary, is
`CyclicGroupFamily.not_ddh_of_even_order` over cyclic group families, with the efficiency
certificate `CyclicGroupFamily.isPPT_ddhParityDistinguisher`. -/
theorem ddhAdvantage_ddhParityDistinguisher (hq : Even q)
    (hg : Function.Injective fun c : ZMod q => c • g) :
    ddhAdvantage q g (ddhParityDistinguisher q) = 1 / 2 := by
  apply NNReal.eq
  rw [ddhAdvantage, Experiment.coe_advantage,
    probability_ddhRealExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher q g hq hg,
    probability_ddhRandomExperiment_ddhParityDistinguisher q g hq hg]
  norm_num

end Experiments

/-! ### The group `(ZMod p)ˣ` -/

section Units

variable (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime]

/-- `p - 1` is nonzero for a prime `p`. Not a global instance; the results below use it as a local
one. -/
theorem neZero_sub_one_of_prime : NeZero (p - 1) :=
  ⟨(Nat.sub_pos_of_lt (Fact.out : p.Prime).one_lt).ne'⟩

/-- The unit group `(ZMod p)ˣ` of a prime `p`, written additively, as a `ZMod (p - 1)`-module:
every unit satisfies `x ^ (p - 1) = 1` (Fermat's little theorem). This is a definition, not a
global instance; clients enable it with `attribute [local instance] zmodUnitsModule` (together
with `neZero_sub_one_of_prime`) or with `letI`. -/
abbrev zmodUnitsModule : Module (ZMod (p - 1)) (Additive (ZMod p)ˣ) :=
  AddCommGroup.zmodModule fun x => by
    rw [← ofMul_toMul x, ← ofMul_pow, ZMod.units_pow_card_sub_one_eq_one, ofMul_one]

attribute [local instance] neZero_sub_one_of_prime zmodUnitsModule

/-- A generator `g` of `(ZMod p)ˣ` has order `p - 1`: `c ↦ c • g` is injective on `ZMod (p - 1)`
in the module structure `zmodUnitsModule p`. -/
theorem injective_smul_ofMul_units {g : (ZMod p)ˣ} (hg : ∀ x, x ∈ Subgroup.zpowers g) :
    Function.Injective fun c : ZMod (p - 1) => c • Additive.ofMul g := by
  have hval (c : ZMod (p - 1)) : c • Additive.ofMul g = c.val • Additive.ofMul g := by
    conv_lhs => rw [← ZMod.natCast_zmod_val c]
    exact Nat.cast_smul_eq_nsmul _ _ _
  have horder : addOrderOf (Additive.ofMul g) = p - 1 := by
    rw [addOrderOf_ofMul_eq_orderOf, orderOf_eq_card_of_forall_mem_zpowers hg,
      Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, ZMod.card_units]
  intro c c' h
  simp only [hval] at h
  rw [nsmul_inj_mod, horder, Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (ZMod.val_lt c),
    Nat.mod_eq_of_lt (ZMod.val_lt c')] at h
  exact ZMod.val_injective _ h

/-- **The parity test in `(ZMod p)ˣ`** ([BonehShoup2023], Section 16.1.3, p. 687): for an odd
prime `p` and any generator `g` of `(ZMod p)ˣ`, a cyclic group of even order `p - 1`, the parity
test of Section 16.1.3 has fixed-group DDH advantage exactly `1 / 2`. The group is written
additively, with the module structure `zmodUnitsModule p`. Generators exist: `(ZMod p)ˣ` is cyclic
(`IsCyclic.exists_generator`). This fixed-group statement does not bound running time, so it
does not prove the book's conclusion that DDH does not hold in `Z_p^*` (p. 687, and the remark
after Exercise 10.24, p. 431), which concerns efficient adversaries. That would follow from
`CyclicGroupFamily.not_ddh_of_even_order` for a family of these groups, which is not constructed
here. -/
theorem ddhAdvantage_units_ddhParityDistinguisher (hp : p ≠ 2) {g : (ZMod p)ˣ}
    (hg : ∀ x, x ∈ Subgroup.zpowers g) :
    ddhAdvantage (p - 1) (Additive.ofMul g) (ddhParityDistinguisher (p - 1)) = 1 / 2 :=
  ddhAdvantage_ddhParityDistinguisher (p - 1) (Additive.ofMul g)
    ((Fact.out : p.Prime).even_sub_one hp) (injective_smul_ofMul_units p hg)

end Units

end Cslib.Crypto
